<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vajrayana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tantric Buddhism, Esoteric Buddhism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vajrayana, or Tantric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, is a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> that developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from around the 7th century, using ritual, mantras, mandalas and initiation by a teacher to seek rapid enlightenment. It spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and maritime South-East Asia, where Javanese and Sumatran kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityawarman">Adityawarman</a> combined it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a> in royal cult and temple building.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vajrayana.jpg" alt="Vajrayana" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pierre Dalbéra from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghanta_et_Vajra_(British_Museum)_(8697431158).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The people are mainly Ngalops and Sharchops, with the Nepali-speaking Lhotshampa in the south; two-thirds to three-quarters follow <b>Vajrayana</b> Buddhism and 22.6% are Hindus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty was Buddhist, its kings styling themselves paramasaugata, devoted followers of the Buddha, and it supported Mahayana and <b>Vajrayana Buddhism</b> on a scale unmatched in India after the Guptas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruler's legitimacy was framed within Mahayana and later <b>Vajrayana Buddhism</b>, and he was probably presented as a figure of great spiritual merit, possibly a bodhisattva, whose authority derived from accumulated virtue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Vajrayana</b>, which arose in India from about the 6th century, added tantric rituals, mantras and visualisation practices taught by a guru.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>The Charyapada is a collection of mystical poems, songs of realisation in the tantric <b>Vajrayana</b> tradition of Buddhism from Assam, Bengal, Bihar and Odisha.</blockquote><a href="/songs/charyapada">Charyapada</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tantric Buddhism</b> flourished at the court.</blockquote><a href="/state/dharmasraya">Dharmasraya</a></li></ul></section>
